For a step boundary change in a homogeneous initially uniform semi-infinite body, the solution depends on x/√(αt). Derive the similarity reduction and error-function profile, then check initial and boundary limits. The diffusion length is a scale, not a sharp front beyond which Fourier's solution is exactly unchanged.

Compare specimen thickness with the penetrated region over the observation time before treating the domain as semi-infinite. A finite pulse, surface resistance, layered material or temperature-dependent property changes the response.

Recover the integrated heat uptake and surface-flux time dependence from the same solution. Use it to test a transient numerical model and to understand why an early-time thermal measurement emphasizes a different region from a late-time measurement. Explain the nonphysical infinite propagation feature of Fourier diffusion and when a more detailed model is needed.

